# The oracle references, pinned, in one image.
#
# **One image rather than one per format, because the questions interlock.**
# These are not tools that each answer about their own format; they are
# independent implementations asked the *same* question about the same
# containers, and the whole value is in where they disagree. If they lived in
# separate images with separate base states, a disagreement would first have to
# be shown not to be the images' fault. One image makes "the reference set" one
# fact.
#
# The directory was `tars/` until zip arrived. It is `refs/` now because the
# image answers about two formats, and a directory named after one of them is a
# claim that stops being true the moment the second reference is installed.
#
# tar       GNU tar. Writes v7, ustar, GNU and pax on demand, so it is the only
#           reference that can produce all four of the tar formats this library
#           has to read. Its --format flag is what the tar corpus is built from.
# bsdtar    libarchive. The second reader, and the one that matters: a corpus
#           generated and read back by GNU tar alone measures GNU tar. bsdtar
#           and GNU tar disagree about real things - which is the point. It
#           reads and writes zip as well, so it is a reference for both formats
#           and the only one that is.
# python3   tarfile and zipfile, a third independent implementation of each, and
#           the one that is scriptable without a shell in the way a metadata
#           comparison needs. tarfile's three `format=` values are USTAR, GNU and
#           PAX; zipfile is the writer that can store an arbitrary member name,
#           which is what a hostile-name fixture needs.
# zip       Info-ZIP's writer, which is what most zip files in the world were
#           written by. Its defaults are the ones a reader meets: a UT extended
#           timestamp extra field, a Unix uid/gid extra field, and `-X` to turn
#           both off - so it is the reference that can produce a member whose
#           only time is the two-second DOS field.
# unzip     Info-ZIP's reader, a *separate program with a separate version* from
#           zip - 6.00 against 3.0 - and the one whose `-t` is the accepted
#           answer to "is this a valid zip".
# 7z        7-Zip, a fourth implementation and the one that writes what the
#           others will not: WinZip AES (method 99) for the refusal phase D owes,
#           and a different `version made by` and extra-field layout from either
#           of the two above. Debian's `7zip` package installs it as `7z`; the
#           upstream name `7zz` is not present, and `p7zip-full` is a
#           transitional package pointing here. Every invocation of it passes
#           `-mmt=1`, because its thread count defaults to the host's core count
#           and a fixture compressed by twelve threads is not the fixture
#           compressed by four.
#
# Everything the build reads is pinned, because an image built here cannot be
# pinned by digest: two builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one
# image. So the base is pinned by digest and the packages by full apt version,
# and the run-time version check in oracle_env.py is the real guarantee - which
# is why check_pin() runs in both modes.
#
# **The apt pins do not expire, because apt is pointed at a dated snapshot.**
# The Debian archive keeps one version of a package and drops it when an update
# lands, so an exact version resolves today and stops resolving in some number of
# weeks. snapshot.debian.org keeps every version ever published. The date is not
# chosen: it is the snapshot the base image itself records in the comments of its
# own /etc/apt/sources.list.d/debian.sources, so every package installed comes
# from the archive state the base was built against.
#
# Two mechanics that are easy to get wrong, taken from compress's image where
# they were found by getting them wrong: the URL must be **http**, because the
# slim base carries no CA certificates and https fails with a certificate error
# that reads like a network fault - apt verifies by signature either way; and
# Check-Valid-Until has to be off, because a dated Release file is stale by
# design.
#
# The same base digest as every other oracle image in the suite: one base to
# pull, and a glibc the host's own binaries can run against.
FROM docker.io/library/debian@sha256:7792b1f7702a86946cd518db72b6a407302c3e9bc1635634368b878189e8221c

# The snapshot the base records; override only to move every pin at once.
ARG SNAPSHOT=20260918T000000Z

RUN printf 'Types: deb\nURIs: http://snapshot.debian.org/archive/debian/%s\nSuites: trixie trixie-updates\nComponents: main\nSigned-By: /usr/share/keyrings/debian-archive-keyring.pgp\n\nTypes: deb\nURIs: http://snapshot.debian.org/archive/debian-security/%s\nSuites: trixie-security\nComponents: main\nSigned-By: /usr/share/keyrings/debian-archive-keyring.pgp\n' \
      "$SNAPSHOT" "$SNAPSHOT" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/debian.sources \
    && printf 'Acquire::Check-Valid-Until "false";\n' \
      > /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99snapshot

RUN apt-get update -qq \
    && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      tar=1.35+dfsg-3.1 \
      libarchive-tools=3.7.4-4+deb13u1 \
      python3=3.13.5-1 \
      zip=3.0-15+deb13u1 \
      unzip=6.0-29+deb13u1 \
      7zip=25.01+dfsg-1~deb13u2 \
      libstdc++6 \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# The probes oracle_env.py runs, copied in rather than written with printf inside
# a RUN: font's first draft did that and its escapes reached the file literally,
# which puts a quoting layer between a check and the fact it checks.
COPY tar-version bsdtar-version pytarfile-version \
      zip-version unzip-version sevenzip-version pyzipfile-version \
      /usr/local/bin/
RUN chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/tar-version /usr/local/bin/bsdtar-version \
      /usr/local/bin/pytarfile-version /usr/local/bin/zip-version \
      /usr/local/bin/unzip-version /usr/local/bin/sevenzip-version \
      /usr/local/bin/pyzipfile-version

# A locale, pinned, because regex lost 1,855 differential rows to an image that
# had none. tar speaks bytes, which is the whole reason this library refuses to
# transcode a name - but zip does not: Info-ZIP's `zip` consults the locale to
# decide whether a name it stores is UTF-8, and therefore whether to set general
# purpose flag bit 11. So for the zip half this is not belt-and-braces, it is an
# input to the fixture, and a fixture whose UTF-8 flag depended on the ambient
# locale would be a different archive on a different machine.
ENV LANG=C.UTF-8 LC_ALL=C.UTF-8

# Reproducible archives need these fixed, and fixing them here rather than in
# the generator means every invocation gets them, including one a person types
# by hand while working out why a fixture moved.
ENV TZ=UTC LC_TIME=C.UTF-8

ENTRYPOINT []
CMD ["sh", "-c", "tar-version; bsdtar-version; pytarfile-version; \
zip-version; unzip-version; sevenzip-version; pyzipfile-version"]
